{"kind":"assert","text":"Advisory criterion for the revision (from the operator): the first implementation must deliver immediate value as a policy-guard analytics foundation — agents doing self-analytics and operators watching a diverse fleet across Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode, including out-of-bounds tool calls/operations and endless-loop or stalled behavior. Assessment of the current draft against that bar: the core reaches it (one normalizer can unify all three harness vocabularies into shared session/tool tables; per-session counters and event-time windows express repeated-failure and loop-suspicion signals; query-time export joins cover fleet views; MCP query covers agent self-analytics), with five gaps builder should address in the revision rather than discover during implementation. (1) Guards are trailing and pull-based: harness OTLP exporters batch (seconds for logs, ~a minute for metrics) and waves add latency, so tailapp can gate the NEXT action, not the current one — document the end-to-end freshness budget, and consider adding to acceptance a sample harness hook (e.g. a PreToolUse hook running a bounded query) proving the guard loop closes. (2) Stalled/idle detection is answerable only at query time in v1 because folds never see wall clock and absence of events produces no fold input; decide explicitly whether a deterministic engine-generated tick record (an ordered inbox record like any other — no dataflow risk, but a new platform input) enters initial scope or stays deferred with the pull-only story stated plainly. (3) Policy signal availability is bounded by harness content gates: at defaults, tool names and success/failure are exported but arguments and prompts largely are not, so argument-level out-of-bounds guards (paths, commands) may be unreachable at default gates — the samples and docs must state which guards work at defaults and which need gates opened. (4) There is no seed-data mechanism in the DDL profile, so policy configuration (allowlists, budgets, thresholds) is necessarily policy-as-code embedded in fold/normalizer sources and updated via draft+continue activation; name that pattern explicitly — and note it sharpens required change R1 from review d6243a36, because policy iteration must never force a data-destroying reset. (5) The shipped samples carry the immediate-value story: alongside tool-activity and session-cost, add a policy-guard sample tailapp (out-of-bounds tool detection against an in-source allowlist, repeated-failure loop suspicion per session, last-activity-per-session for stall queries) and include it in end-to-end acceptance."}